 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - - 
 introduction.
 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -

 included in this archive is the complete source code for starplayer 2.25,
 including the music library, cd audio and all interface code. i am assuming
 you are already familiar with what starplayer is and what its main features
 are (and are familiar/experienced with tran's pmode extender). if you
 aren't then you should grab starp225.zip and read starplay.doc which comes
 with that (this documentation is also included in this archive if you have
 trouble finding it). this archive contains the release version executable and
 can be found at a number of sites including:

	ftp://ftp.cdrom.com/pub/demos/music/programs/players/starp225.zip
	ftp://cygnus.uwa.edu.au/pub/people/jedi/starp225.zip

 also, if you have a unix shell account you might be able to get a copy from
 my .plan file by typing:

	finger jedi@tartarus.uwa.edu.au | uudecode

 basically for those who (still) don't know, starplayer is a protected mode
 s3m/mtm/mod/cd player for gravis ultrasound and soundblaster soundcards.
 it lets you load several modules into memory at once and flip between them
 quickly in a dos shell. it has a nifty popup menu with file selector so you
 can find and load new modules from within other dos programs. it can also
 operate as a mtm/mod -> s3m module format converter and has lots of other
 neat things such as ctrl-alt-del crash recovery and support for loading
 modules from filelist files. as this is a source code release i'm not going
 to cover how to use all these features and you'll just have to get the
 executable release (starp225.zip) mentioned above.

 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -
 the source code
 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -

 the source code consists of the following files:

	 starplay.asm - main program user interface code (popup menus, file
			selection, status bars, etc)
	   s3mlib.asm - music library code (s3m playback code, loading code,
			mtm/mod conversion code)
	 cdromlib.asm - cd audio library code (requires mscdex driver)

 the other two files, pmode.asm and file.asm are part of the pmode 2.51
 distribution by thomas pytel (a.k.a tran). they are included so the player
 can be assembled straight away in case you don't already have them.
 (you should have these files anyway if you are familiar with programming
 using pmode).

 the include files s3mlib.inc and cdromlib.inc contain the public function
 definitions and are required to compile starplayer. you will also need to
 include these into your main program if you wish to use the music library.
 the files pmode.inc and file.inc are required by any pmode programs.

 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -
 assembling
 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -

 to assemble starplayer from this source code you will require turbo assembler
 (tasm.exe, version 4.0 recommended, older versions may also work) and the
 standard linker (link.exe) which comes with dos (or tlink if you prefer).

 assemble starplayer simply by running makesp.bat at the dos prompt. if you
 want to make the executable as small as the release version (~25k) then use
 an exe compressor such as pklite.

 note: a precompiled executable (starplay.exe) is present in this archive so
 you can look at the program even if you havn't got tasm.

 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -
 using the music library in your own programs
 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -

 this is probably what most of you will want to do. the basic procedure is:

	include the s3mlib function declarations in your code32 segment:
		include pmode.inc               ;required for pmode programs
		include s3mlib.inc

	initialise the sound system with device information.
	first, set the global soundcard addressing variables:
		mov [__PortAddr],PORT           ;set soundcard port
		mov [__IntNum],IRQ              ;set soundcard irq
		mov [__DMAChan],DMA             ;set soundcard dma channel
	then call the initialisation procedure:
		mov al,[ForceCard]
		mov bx,[Default_MixRate]
		mov dx,[Default_BufLen]
		call PM_InitSystem
		jc error                        ;error if carry flag is set
	ForceCard contains the soundcard device number, usually set
	this to DEVICE_AUTO to autodetect
	(can also be DEVICE_GUS or DEVICE_SBMONO)
	the port, irq and dma values can be read from the ULTRASND= and
	BLASTER= environment variables...see starplay.asm for an example.

	load the module to play and get a handle for it
		mov ebx,[Handle]
		mov al,LOAD_DUMP+LOAD_HI        ;Load module into hi mem,
						;then dump samples to device
		mov edx,offset Filename
		call PM_LoadModule
		jc error
	Handle is a pointer to an empty structure of M_Struc_Size bytes.
	Must have enough room here to store the entire structure before the
	PM_LoadModule function is called.
	Carry flag is set if there is an error loading the module (eg. file
	type is not recognised)

	note: there is another useful function PM_GetFileType which can be
	used to quickly identify the type of module given by a particular
	filename without loading it. the calling procedure for this is as
	follows:
		mov edx,offset Filename         ;filename to examine
		mov edi,offset Title            ;28 chars to fill with title
		call PM_GetFileType
		jc error                        ;carry set if not valid module
						;  or if file error
	on return, al register contains: 0 if not recognised
		       or TYPE_S3M, TYPE_MTM, TYPE_MOD accordingly
	and the memory pointed to by edi will be filled with the module title.
	this function is used by the file selector in starplayer to identify
	possible modules and extract the title.

	start playing the module using this code:
		mov al,[ForceVol]               ;almost always set to zero
		mov ebx,[Handle]
		call PM_PlayModule
	the ForceVol value is used to force soundblaster mixing amplification
	different to that specified in the module being played. this normally
	isn't required so set it to zero.
	Handle is a pointer to the structure filled by PM_LoadModule

	you can stop playback using PM_StopModule
		call PM_StopModule

	you can get/set the master module volume once a module is playing
	by using PM_GetMasterVol and PM_SetMasterVol
		call PM_GetMasterVol            ;returns current volume in al
	and
		mov al,[MasterVol]              ;volume between 0 and 64
		call PM_SetMasterVol

	finally, before you exit the program make sure you call
	PM_CloseSystem to properly reset the sound card and tidy up
		call PM_CloseSystem
	this will automatically call PM_StopModule if required.

 that covers the basic procedure for loading and playing modules. if you want
 to do things such as changing the current pattern/row/tempo etc or get info
 for volume bars etc then you will need to read/modify the module handle
 data structure directly (see Module struc in s3mlib.inc). starplay.asm has
 several examples on how to do this.

 the global variable __CurrentModule is a pointer to the currently playing
 Module struc and the variable __ChannelData is a pointer to 32 consecutive
 ChannelData strucs, one for each channel of the current module. be careful
 when messing with the data in these structures.

 there are a few other music library functions which may be useful. they are:
	PM_GetDeviceRAM - returns the amount of free ram remaining on the
		current sound device (free gus dram or system ram for sb).
		this function sets the global variable __DeviceRAM accordingly
		and returns carry set if there is an error.
	PM_ReleaseModule - call this to release device ram (ie. free gus dram)
		edx = handle for module to release
		al = 0 to free device ram or non-zero to also free system ram
			(ie. remove module from memory entirely)
		note: this function is very limited! it MUST be called in
		the reverse order which modules are loaded or device samples
		may get corrupted. it releases system memory simply by
		increasing the pmode free memory counter which is a very nasty
		hack so make sure you don't allocate any memory in between
		loading and releasing modules and make sure modules are freed
		in the reverse order to how they were loaded. this function
		and the lack of proper memory management is the cause of a lot
		of troubles with the 'r' key in the popup menu.
	PM_SetLoopCode - use this to set an optional function which will be
		called whenever a module loops back to the beginning.
		starplayer uses this to start playing the next module in the
		list when one finishes.
		call with edx=pointer to near code32 routine
		this routine must preserve all registers and return with a
		normal ret instruction. see starplay.asm for an example.

 assembling and linking is straightforward - take a look at makesp.bat

 using cdromlib can be fairly tricky thanks to the complexity of the mscdex
 api so your best bet is to wade through starplay.asm and pick out what you
 can. cdromlib.asm contains function definitions and information regarding
 parameter passing if you need more details. you will need to include
 cdromlib.inc into your code32 segment and link with cdromlib.obj

 an interesting thing to do is to uncomment the SETRASTERON and SETRASTEROFF
 macros in s3mlib.asm (lines 4192, 4329, 5587 and 5652). this will change
 the value of screen colour 0 whenever the music library irq is executing and
 can be used to get a rough idea of how much cpu time is being dedicated to
 playing music.

 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -
 summary
 ------------------------------- -------- ----- - -

 this source code has been sitting on my harddrive untouched for almost a
 year now so i have decided to release it for others to use and learn from.
 most of it is very messy and poorly designed (especially the user interface
 stuff) but it is functional and for simple music in pmode programs it is
 often ideal. i stopped working on this code when i decided to make the
 switch from pmode 2.51 to pmode/w to overcome some of the limitations of the
 old pmode code and hence no work has been done on it since. a lot of the
 code has been entirely rewritten using the new extender but it is still not
 finished thanks to university commitments.

 since then i have postponed that idea indefinately while i work on a module
 player for linux. the new design allows for playback over multiple devices
 and the playback of multiple modules (by one or more processes) on the one
 device. eventually i hope to port this player to dos when it may become the
 next major version of starplayer and will have all the features (such as
 xm/it etc) which are still lacking. as such there probably won't be another
 starplayer using this code base.

 feel free to use this code in your own demos/intros/musicdisks/etc. if you
 use it for anything i am interested in seeing it. an email or a letter to the
 address below and a word in the credits would make me really happy. if you
 make any bugfixes or add any new features to this code please pass them on to
 me and i'll release a new starplayer and give you the appropriate credit.

